WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
How's lunch?

00:06.000 --> 00:08.000
Good.

00:08.000 --> 00:11.000
More importantly, how you're keeping your well.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.000
Yeah, it's good stuff.

00:15.000 --> 00:17.000
Just that curiosity, what was for lunch?

00:17.000 --> 00:19.000
When people feed back, one of the things they always feed back on.

00:19.000 --> 00:21.000
I was just looking at a feedback for a conference.

00:21.000 --> 00:23.000
That was that for you.

00:23.000 --> 00:24.000
That conference is shall remain nameless.

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
And one of the things people critique was the lunch was terrible.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
I'm hoping your lunch was really good.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
And if nothing else, it buys me a couple of seconds to kind of feel what's

00:35.000 --> 00:36.000
happening in this space.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
So feel very fortunate to be here, really fortunate to be here.

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
And we find myself in a very interesting time at the moment,

00:45.000 --> 00:46.000
really interesting time.

00:46.000 --> 00:51.000
And it's amazing kind of where we have found ourselves.

00:51.000 --> 00:55.000
Just even in the last five years, let alone the last decade.

00:55.000 --> 01:00.000
But I think one of the things that's always interesting to think

01:00.000 --> 01:04.000
about is kind of what my dad refers to as the mundanity of life.

01:04.000 --> 01:06.000
So the simple things that make people happy.

01:06.000 --> 01:09.000
And what the greatest sites are around that.

01:09.000 --> 01:18.000
And for me, personally speaking, the greatest site that I see is a kid in a park.

01:18.000 --> 01:21.000
Completely free, emancipated, unencumbered.

01:21.000 --> 01:22.000
They're just free.

01:22.000 --> 01:24.000
They're just playing free.

01:24.000 --> 01:28.000
And as you get older, you lose that for a lot of reasons.

01:28.000 --> 01:30.000
You know, life wears on you.

01:30.000 --> 01:32.000
You get these residual scars.

01:32.000 --> 01:36.000
You know, you don't kind of fly across the monkey bars as you might do.

01:36.000 --> 01:40.000
You're a bit more attentive about what you may do because you don't hurt yourself.

01:40.000 --> 01:45.000
And when I kind of think of that freedom and that belief to hit free as a child,

01:45.000 --> 01:49.000
I kind of think what arrests that as you become older.

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
And then if you kind of narrow that even more as kind of a guest

01:52.000 --> 01:56.000
researchers do, you kind of think about spaces.

01:56.000 --> 02:01.000
And I guess in this context we're talking about a classroom space.

02:01.000 --> 02:06.000
And the freedom and the artistry that, you know, a pedagode may have

02:06.000 --> 02:09.000
in using all of their arsenal and just being that kid in a park,

02:09.000 --> 02:12.000
to being free, just being able to teach unencumbered,

02:12.000 --> 02:15.000
emancipated, free, that freedom.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.000
And you kind of think what restricts that.

02:17.000 --> 02:20.000
When you watch that, well, when you see it, it's poetry emotion.

02:20.000 --> 02:24.000
You look at it and you think, oh my God, you have taken the most difficult concept

02:24.000 --> 02:27.000
and you've made it so easy to understand.

02:27.000 --> 02:31.000
And I always had this idea around when I used to observe certain people,

02:31.000 --> 02:33.000
you know, in, you watch lots of keynotes.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
You obviously, you attend lots of lectures, all of these kind of things.

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
And it wasn't a person, yes, obviously mastery of theory

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
and all of these things is really, really important.

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
But the person that I gravitated towards the most,

02:43.000 --> 02:45.000
I suppose you had the mastery of all of that,

02:45.000 --> 02:48.000
but could take a simple message to convey in a very simple way.

02:48.000 --> 02:51.000
And it was really funny during the pandemic.

02:51.000 --> 02:54.000
I think his name was Professor Vantam.

02:54.000 --> 02:57.000
Can't remember his name.

02:57.000 --> 02:59.000
Vantam, but he had, he kind of garnered a reputation

02:59.000 --> 03:03.000
for basically taking really complex messages

03:03.000 --> 03:06.000
and making them really simple for the general public to understand.

03:06.000 --> 03:09.000
So I think famously he said, look, we're free now down

03:09.000 --> 03:12.000
and, you know, we're going to work our way back into this,

03:12.000 --> 03:15.000
you know, but it's going to be tight in terms of talking about COVID.

03:15.000 --> 03:18.000
And you're kind of thinking that someone at the highest level

03:18.000 --> 03:20.000
of kind of trying to explain these things,

03:20.000 --> 03:24.000
but it was very simple analogy that most people could kind of grasp

03:24.000 --> 03:26.000
and they could, you know, that praxis element.

03:26.000 --> 03:30.000
And I guess the difficulty of being in these spaces now,

03:30.000 --> 03:34.000
besides performance indicators,

03:34.000 --> 03:37.000
are that there are pressures on people which mean they can't hit free.

03:37.000 --> 03:40.000
They can't be that kid in the park in the classroom space.

03:40.000 --> 03:44.000
And when you think about it and you see it, it's restricting.

03:44.000 --> 03:47.000
You know, and I had a friend who wants to explain to me,

03:47.000 --> 03:50.000
you know, Jason, being a lecturer in higher education now

03:50.000 --> 03:53.000
is like kind of, it's like trying to do something

03:53.000 --> 03:55.000
with two hands tied behind your back.

03:55.000 --> 03:57.000
You can't be as you were.

03:57.000 --> 03:58.000
There's too many things you have to think about.

03:58.000 --> 04:01.000
You have to think about students as faction.

04:01.000 --> 04:03.000
You have to think about if you're kind of thinking broadly

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
in terms of the intersection.

04:05.000 --> 04:08.000
You know, my friend who said this to me just to be clear

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
was a woman of color.

04:10.000 --> 04:12.000
And she made it very clear to me that there are certain things

04:12.000 --> 04:15.000
that I have to think about in the classroom space.

04:15.000 --> 04:18.000
I can't just go in there and just kind of shoot

04:18.000 --> 04:21.000
from the hip so to speak, pedagogically speaking.

04:21.000 --> 04:25.000
And said all of these factors become really decisive anchors

04:25.000 --> 04:28.000
and levers in terms of the decision-making I make,

04:28.000 --> 04:30.000
which means that I'm not free when I'm doing it.

04:30.000 --> 04:32.000
And sometimes I feel like I'm being quite robotic.

04:32.000 --> 04:35.000
I'm doing everything they need.

04:35.000 --> 04:38.000
But am I giving them what they want?

04:38.000 --> 04:40.000
Because when it comes to it, I'm going to say

04:40.000 --> 04:42.000
if there's any complaints about me that, well,

04:42.000 --> 04:44.000
I've done what I was supposed to do.

04:44.000 --> 04:46.000
And I guess is that whole idea of what do people need

04:46.000 --> 04:48.000
and what do people want?

04:48.000 --> 04:50.000
And look, with these things,

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
there's all the privilege to touch to it.

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
You know, who is allowed to belong in a classroom space?

04:55.000 --> 04:56.000
Who is allowed to be expressive?

04:56.000 --> 05:00.000
Who is allowed to exercise freedom of speech?

05:00.000 --> 05:03.000
And I say that because I think we're living in a time

05:03.000 --> 05:07.000
where people abuse that most precious of privileges,

05:07.000 --> 05:09.000
freedom of speech, people abuse it.

05:09.000 --> 05:11.000
And in the classroom space,

05:11.000 --> 05:13.000
if we kind of believe what we hear,

05:13.000 --> 05:16.000
that's where we're kind of brainwashing all of these people

05:16.000 --> 05:18.000
who are going to the labor market

05:18.000 --> 05:21.000
and then speak this utter woke nonsense.

05:21.000 --> 05:24.000
I don't think like that, but this I'm paranoid.

05:24.000 --> 05:26.000
And it's interesting.

05:26.000 --> 05:29.000
It's just interesting to kind of see where we are.

05:29.000 --> 05:33.000
And I think all of it comes back to this central seed of

05:33.000 --> 05:36.000
values and belief systems,

05:36.000 --> 05:37.000
values and belief systems,

05:37.000 --> 05:39.000
in any kind of teacher training program.

05:39.000 --> 05:41.000
I think the first thing you're taught is

05:41.000 --> 05:45.000
what are your values and belief systems as a potential teacher?

05:45.000 --> 05:49.000
And for whatever reason, and it happens to all of us,

05:49.000 --> 05:53.000
it can be quite remarkable how far you can be made to stray away

05:53.000 --> 05:56.000
from those values and belief systems.

05:56.000 --> 05:58.000
One, through compliance,

05:58.000 --> 06:01.000
two, because the landscape of higher education

06:01.000 --> 06:04.000
is very, very different in terms of what students expect

06:04.000 --> 06:07.000
and the consumerist nature of it kind of takes away

06:07.000 --> 06:09.000
the soul of it.

06:09.000 --> 06:12.000
But again, it goes back to that kid in the park.

06:12.000 --> 06:16.000
When you meet an adult who is silly,

06:16.000 --> 06:18.000
the first thing I don't think is why you're silly.

06:18.000 --> 06:19.000
The first thing I think is,

06:19.000 --> 06:22.000
how have you managed to keep that childlike hubris?

06:22.000 --> 06:23.000
How have you managed to not?

06:23.000 --> 06:27.000
How has life managed not to get to you for you to keep that?

06:27.000 --> 06:30.000
And then when I think of someone I look at in a classroom space

06:30.000 --> 06:32.000
and you see the energy, you see the verb,

06:32.000 --> 06:34.000
you see all of it together, you think,

06:34.000 --> 06:36.000
how do people manage to do that?

06:36.000 --> 06:38.000
But then from one classroom to next, increasingly,

06:38.000 --> 06:41.000
you're seeing that people are struggling more and more

06:41.000 --> 06:42.000
to keep that.

06:42.000 --> 06:44.000
And I guess the question is why?

06:44.000 --> 06:46.000
Why?

06:46.000 --> 06:49.000
And it kind of refers me back to one of the important

06:49.000 --> 06:51.000
and the most important things that I think we all have

06:51.000 --> 06:54.000
as individuals, which I think is agency.

06:54.000 --> 06:58.000
You know, there is a privilege attached to agency of course,

06:58.000 --> 07:02.000
but it's that idea of this is my playground.

07:02.000 --> 07:04.000
This is my domain.

07:04.000 --> 07:08.000
Now, what you can't be afraid to do is to hit for the lines.

07:08.000 --> 07:11.000
So at the moment, I guess as a sector,

07:11.000 --> 07:15.000
you know, we've all been corralled into hitting inside the lines.

07:15.000 --> 07:17.000
Right, none of us are hitting big anymore.

07:17.000 --> 07:19.000
I'm generalizing for the purpose of this conversation,

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
but we're not hitting big anymore.

07:21.000 --> 07:22.000
We're playing it safe.

07:22.000 --> 07:24.000
You know, will we get a ball backing court?

07:24.000 --> 07:25.000
Yes.

07:25.000 --> 07:26.000
Will the ball come back to us?

07:26.000 --> 07:27.000
Yes.

07:27.000 --> 07:29.000
But it gets to a stage where you think, okay,

07:29.000 --> 07:30.000
do I want to hit for the lines?

07:30.000 --> 07:32.000
Do I want to be the person to take the point?

07:32.000 --> 07:35.000
Or do I want to be dictated to in terms of what I can do?

07:35.000 --> 07:38.000
Now, there's a lot of idealism attached to what I'm saying,

07:38.000 --> 07:41.000
but it is situated in this idea of hope and the idea

07:41.000 --> 07:46.000
that actually our pedagogies and the way we operate in these spaces

07:46.000 --> 07:51.000
are something much more powerful than just being at the front

07:51.000 --> 07:52.000
and doing something.

07:52.000 --> 07:54.000
You know, these things shape people's lives.

07:54.000 --> 07:57.000
They shape people's understanding of the world that they eventually

07:57.000 --> 07:59.000
will be contributing to.

07:59.000 --> 08:02.000
And that charge is so important.

08:02.000 --> 08:05.000
It's so important that we have to kind of protect that

08:05.000 --> 08:06.000
as much as possible.

08:06.000 --> 08:10.000
Now, how do we protect that in the higher education sector

08:10.000 --> 08:12.000
that we find ourselves in now?

08:12.000 --> 08:15.000
I think the idea of vulnerability in those spaces

08:15.000 --> 08:17.000
becomes really, really important.

08:17.000 --> 08:22.000
And part of it is an acceptance that actually

08:22.000 --> 08:25.000
knowledge construction is a two-way streak.

08:25.000 --> 08:28.000
So, for example, the premise that I always work from

08:28.000 --> 08:32.000
is that yes, I am being self-deprecating,

08:32.000 --> 08:34.000
but it is true.

08:34.000 --> 08:35.000
99% of the time.

08:35.000 --> 08:38.000
The people that I've talked throughout my career

08:38.000 --> 08:40.000
are a lot more intelligent than I am.

08:40.000 --> 08:43.000
I know a lot about a very small area.

08:43.000 --> 08:45.000
And the great thing about being a student,

08:45.000 --> 08:47.000
as we've all been, most of us,

08:47.000 --> 08:51.000
is that you learn a lot, you know, along this wide spectrum

08:51.000 --> 08:54.000
and then if you go into academia, you get to more narrow

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
and narrow and narrow and narrow.

08:56.000 --> 08:58.000
But the essence of having that broad structure

08:58.000 --> 09:00.000
is really, really important.

09:00.000 --> 09:03.000
And I guess for people who are,

09:03.000 --> 09:04.000
when you're teaching people,

09:04.000 --> 09:06.000
one of the things you're thinking is right,

09:06.000 --> 09:08.000
is there an opportunity for,

09:08.000 --> 09:10.000
I always have this idea that if someone walks in

09:10.000 --> 09:12.000
or looks peaks through the window,

09:12.000 --> 09:14.000
I don't want them to be able to know

09:14.000 --> 09:17.000
who the person conducting the learning is

09:17.000 --> 09:20.000
because the idea is to facilitate that space.

09:20.000 --> 09:22.000
But now someone might look at that and kind of think,

09:22.000 --> 09:25.000
well, that shows a lack of control

09:25.000 --> 09:28.000
or, you know, exercising pedagogy in the sense

09:28.000 --> 09:31.000
that actually I'm here to teach and you learn.

09:31.000 --> 09:34.000
But for me, it's that idea of how do we take something,

09:34.000 --> 09:37.000
you know, as transient as pedagogy

09:37.000 --> 09:40.000
and make it as just normal as anything,

09:40.000 --> 09:42.000
a conversation between two people,

09:42.000 --> 09:44.000
a conversation between a group of people,

09:44.000 --> 09:48.000
recognizing, recognizing that when you go into any space,

09:48.000 --> 09:50.000
you work from the presumption, the generous presumption

09:50.000 --> 09:52.000
that people know.

09:52.000 --> 09:55.000
So one of the things that I deliberately don't do

09:55.000 --> 09:57.000
is that I don't use slides.

09:57.000 --> 09:59.000
One, because I don't know who I'm talking to,

09:59.000 --> 10:01.000
that's the first thing.

10:01.000 --> 10:02.000
Two, because I don't work on the presumption

10:02.000 --> 10:04.000
that people don't know.

10:04.000 --> 10:06.000
Now, why does that become hugely important?

10:06.000 --> 10:08.000
Because if we look across the intersection,

10:08.000 --> 10:11.000
those types of deficit ways of thinking

10:11.000 --> 10:14.000
often apply to minoritized groups.

10:14.000 --> 10:16.000
So the presumption is you don't know

10:16.000 --> 10:19.000
or you are not as competent or you're not as able.

10:19.000 --> 10:22.000
And we could do that across the spectrum

10:22.000 --> 10:24.000
because that is true.

10:24.000 --> 10:27.000
But the most important thing is working with that generous assumption

10:27.000 --> 10:28.000
that people know.

10:28.000 --> 10:30.000
And then it's feeling your way through it.

10:30.000 --> 10:33.000
But to do it, you have to trust yourself.

10:33.000 --> 10:35.000
Now, I'm not saying I'm great.

10:35.000 --> 10:37.000
I'm not, if anything, as my friends always say,

10:37.000 --> 10:40.000
Jason is the most underwhelming person you'll ever meet,

10:40.000 --> 10:42.000
which is true in part.

10:42.000 --> 10:45.000
But it's also mainly for the fact that I watch home in a way

10:45.000 --> 10:48.000
and I'm very proud of that.

10:48.000 --> 10:49.000
So that's part of their us and out.

10:49.000 --> 10:52.000
But one of the things that is really, really important

10:52.000 --> 10:55.000
is that idea of how do we trust ourself in these spaces?

10:55.000 --> 10:57.000
How do we trust ourself in these spaces

10:57.000 --> 10:59.000
when you have all of these things coming in?

10:59.000 --> 11:02.000
The societal context, the political context,

11:02.000 --> 11:06.000
the institutional context, the policing of particular individuals

11:06.000 --> 11:10.000
in the institutional context, the policing of how we engage

11:10.000 --> 11:12.000
in these types of pedagogies?

11:12.000 --> 11:14.000
How do we actually create this collective

11:14.000 --> 11:16.000
that we're looking for?

11:16.000 --> 11:19.000
And I think a huge part of it is the human element.

11:19.000 --> 11:22.000
I think the human element has been extracted

11:22.000 --> 11:24.000
from these pedagogical spaces.

11:24.000 --> 11:27.000
And by the way, the reasons are absolutely legitimate.

11:27.000 --> 11:30.000
You know, universities have become factories

11:30.000 --> 11:34.000
and I think we all can accept that and we understand that.

11:34.000 --> 11:37.000
But there is that regard action there

11:37.000 --> 11:39.000
that we can push back against some of these things.

11:39.000 --> 11:42.000
And the reason why I say that is because it goes back

11:42.000 --> 11:44.000
to that values and belief system.

11:44.000 --> 11:47.000
What is the reason I actually became an educator

11:47.000 --> 11:48.000
in the first place?

11:48.000 --> 11:50.000
What is the reason?

11:50.000 --> 11:52.000
Sometimes it's not the most complex things.

11:52.000 --> 11:53.000
It's the most simple things.

11:53.000 --> 11:56.000
Why did I become an educator in the first place?

11:56.000 --> 11:59.000
A few months ago, I was saying to myself,

11:59.000 --> 12:02.000
I was teaching a lesson

12:02.000 --> 12:05.000
and everyone left the group

12:05.000 --> 12:08.000
and a student came to me.

12:08.000 --> 12:10.000
And I'd say so much wrong.

12:10.000 --> 12:12.000
And I said to the student,

12:12.000 --> 12:15.000
I don't know how to explain it.

12:15.000 --> 12:17.000
I don't really think it's appropriate for me

12:17.000 --> 12:19.000
to tell you what I'm thinking.

12:19.000 --> 12:22.000
And I'd go on and I said to them,

12:22.000 --> 12:24.000
that was the brown stuff.

12:24.000 --> 12:26.000
And they were like, what?

12:26.000 --> 12:28.000
I was like, that was crap.

12:28.000 --> 12:31.000
And I was like, well, I think it was crap.

12:31.000 --> 12:35.000
I was like, I said,

12:35.000 --> 12:37.000
do you want to hit free?

12:37.000 --> 12:39.000
You want to be free.

12:39.000 --> 12:42.000
Like, I'm succumbing to something that I'm not.

12:42.000 --> 12:45.000
I'm doing the things that I'm supposed to be doing.

12:45.000 --> 12:47.000
But that's not where you're living.

12:47.000 --> 12:49.000
Being in this space, being an educator,

12:49.000 --> 12:51.000
being in that space, it's like living.

12:51.000 --> 12:55.000
I said, it's another life I'd love to be a rock star.

12:55.000 --> 12:56.000
That will never happen.

12:56.000 --> 12:58.000
But this is the closest thing you get to being one.

12:58.000 --> 13:00.000
And I said, you've got to bring people with you.

13:00.000 --> 13:03.000
And that was just a lesson in how not to do it.

13:03.000 --> 13:05.000
And I was like, well, you were teaching research methods

13:05.000 --> 13:07.000
and I was like, yeah, I know.

13:07.000 --> 13:09.000
But yeah, I know.

13:09.000 --> 13:10.000
I know.

13:10.000 --> 13:11.000
I know.

13:11.000 --> 13:12.000
I know.

13:12.000 --> 13:13.000
I know.

13:13.000 --> 13:14.000
I know.

13:14.000 --> 13:15.000
I know.

13:15.000 --> 13:16.000
It says more about me.

13:16.000 --> 13:18.000
But I was kind of saying to them,

13:18.000 --> 13:19.000
there's ways to make this,

13:19.000 --> 13:21.000
and by the way, I was teaching something that should really

13:21.000 --> 13:22.000
be of massive interest to me.

13:22.000 --> 13:25.000
And it was around ethnographies.

13:25.000 --> 13:30.000
But for whatever reason, though, just wasn't there.

13:30.000 --> 13:31.000
It wasn't there.

13:31.000 --> 13:34.000
And it wasn't like it was just one bad show.

13:34.000 --> 13:35.000
It was several bad shows.

13:35.000 --> 13:38.000
And I was thinking, on the stub hub,

13:38.000 --> 13:40.000
these people need their money back.

13:40.000 --> 13:42.000
And I asked myself, why?

13:42.000 --> 13:43.000
Why?

13:43.000 --> 13:46.000
And what were the people to guide me in steering me

13:46.000 --> 13:47.000
under the right direction?

13:47.000 --> 13:51.000
I had an innocuous conversation with my adult daughter.

13:51.000 --> 13:53.000
I was not particularly interested.

13:53.000 --> 13:55.000
But my 11-year-old was.

13:55.000 --> 13:58.000
And I'd picked him up from football.

13:58.000 --> 14:01.000
And I said to him, he asked me, he said, as I always was.

14:01.000 --> 14:03.000
I said, did you express yourself today now?

14:03.000 --> 14:04.000
I said, yes, I did.

14:04.000 --> 14:05.000
He said, did you express yourself?

14:05.000 --> 14:08.000
I said, not so much, not so much my dog wasn't.

14:08.000 --> 14:10.000
I was a bit rubbish today, to be honest.

14:10.000 --> 14:11.000
And he said, why was you rubbish?

14:11.000 --> 14:14.000
And I said, I taught some lessons and just flat.

14:14.000 --> 14:16.000
Not very good, blah, blah, blah.

14:16.000 --> 14:18.000
And he said, do you know what?

14:18.000 --> 14:20.000
Daddy, you always talk about hitting free.

14:20.000 --> 14:21.000
Did you hit free?

14:21.000 --> 14:22.000
Did you express yourself?

14:22.000 --> 14:24.000
I said, no, not really.

14:24.000 --> 14:25.000
He said, what are your afraid of?

14:25.000 --> 14:27.000
You always say to me what you're afraid of.

14:27.000 --> 14:29.000
And I said, I don't like it in trouble.

14:29.000 --> 14:32.000
And he was like, you get in trouble at your age.

14:32.000 --> 14:37.000
I was like, no, I'm the very scarediest person

14:37.000 --> 14:39.000
of getting in trouble.

14:39.000 --> 14:41.000
I said, you think when I tell you off, it's bad.

14:41.000 --> 14:43.000
I said, listen, you've got a whole lifetime to get used

14:43.000 --> 14:45.000
to being told by someone.

14:45.000 --> 14:48.000
And he said, and he just said something really simple.

14:48.000 --> 14:51.000
Why do you worry about getting told off?

14:51.000 --> 14:53.000
I said, because it's consequences.

14:53.000 --> 14:56.000
And he said, yeah, but you always wanted to be an educator

14:56.000 --> 14:58.000
from what you said to me.

14:58.000 --> 14:59.000
And you're doing something you love.

14:59.000 --> 15:04.000
So why you think, why are you scared of getting told off?

15:04.000 --> 15:08.000
And I thought, and then the next conversation was

15:08.000 --> 15:09.000
that what we had for dinner.

15:09.000 --> 15:11.000
And I was like McDonald's.

15:11.000 --> 15:12.000
Because a good one was cooked.

15:12.000 --> 15:15.000
But then I thought to myself, yeah, why am I scared?

15:15.000 --> 15:16.000
Why am I scared?

15:16.000 --> 15:21.000
And you kind of think, why don't people teach in that free way

15:21.000 --> 15:24.000
that kind of emancipatory free, unencumbered way?

15:24.000 --> 15:28.000
And the big part of it is because like a kid in the park,

15:28.000 --> 15:31.000
today I know what you become, you're scared of getting in trouble.

15:31.000 --> 15:35.000
If you do something wrong, a student can write to the module leader,

15:35.000 --> 15:38.000
then you get that kind of interesting email which says,

15:38.000 --> 15:40.000
dear Jason, I hope you're well.

15:40.000 --> 15:45.000
Well, it doesn't ever say it's just dear Jason, comma.

15:45.000 --> 15:48.000
If possible, just like to have a quick word with you.

15:48.000 --> 15:51.000
And you know where it's going.

15:51.000 --> 15:53.000
You know where it's going.

15:53.000 --> 15:56.000
And it's always that a couple of students

15:56.000 --> 15:59.000
and a couple of students is like one.

15:59.000 --> 16:02.000
And you kind of think to yourself like, and those emails,

16:02.000 --> 16:05.000
like what they become as a reference point,

16:05.000 --> 16:08.000
they become a reference point for basically making yourself

16:08.000 --> 16:11.000
really, really small in these spaces

16:11.000 --> 16:13.000
and just kind of doing what you need to do.

16:13.000 --> 16:16.000
But it's that whole thing of actually what would make me big

16:16.000 --> 16:17.000
in these spaces.

16:17.000 --> 16:22.000
And the big part of it is to almost let go of that fear of failure.

16:22.000 --> 16:26.000
If I get it wrong, you know, I'd rather, you know,

16:26.000 --> 16:29.000
I'd rather get it wrong trying to be as expansive,

16:29.000 --> 16:32.000
hitting and doing it the right way than getting it wrong.

16:32.000 --> 16:34.000
And someone might say, well, that's a costly endeavor, Jason.

16:34.000 --> 16:37.000
That's really costly endeavor because we're dealing with students

16:37.000 --> 16:39.000
and if you get it wrong, it's consequential.

16:39.000 --> 16:41.000
But it's not because I do genuinely believe

16:41.000 --> 16:43.000
that students pick up on these things.

16:43.000 --> 16:48.000
And I also think there is, as custodians of the academy,

16:48.000 --> 16:53.000
there is an expectation that what we provide them with

16:53.000 --> 16:56.000
goes beyond the classroom space.

16:56.000 --> 16:59.000
I do think there is a responsibility there

16:59.000 --> 17:04.000
to contribute towards producing good citizens

17:04.000 --> 17:08.000
and systems that can contribute to a multi-cultural,

17:08.000 --> 17:12.000
multi-ethnic, multi-diverse society in a myriad of ways.

17:12.000 --> 17:15.000
And just beyond theories, they actually have to be able

17:15.000 --> 17:19.000
to know how to apply those theories in a real-life context.

17:19.000 --> 17:25.000
And so a huge part of this is being able to use your full repertoire

17:25.000 --> 17:27.000
of instruments.

17:27.000 --> 17:29.000
You know, how do we do it?

17:29.000 --> 17:32.000
How do we use all of those things within our toolkit?

17:32.000 --> 17:35.000
And part of it is being that kid in the park,

17:35.000 --> 17:38.000
not being afraid to make mistakes,

17:38.000 --> 17:41.000
not having this expectation that actually, you know,

17:41.000 --> 17:44.000
if I make a mistake it's seen as weakness,

17:44.000 --> 17:46.000
or it's seen as maybe I'm not knowledgeable.

17:46.000 --> 17:49.000
The other side of that is that there are,

17:49.000 --> 17:52.000
particularly ethnic minorities on the sharper end of this,

17:52.000 --> 17:56.000
that deficit approach positions them as not being as competent

17:56.000 --> 17:58.000
or not being as able.

17:58.000 --> 18:01.000
And again, it's why I work with the premise that you walk

18:01.000 --> 18:04.000
into a space and you just work with the generous assumption

18:04.000 --> 18:07.000
that people know, you know, it's the greatest respect

18:07.000 --> 18:09.000
I think you can pay people.

18:09.000 --> 18:13.000
And I guess for people across the intersection,

18:13.000 --> 18:16.000
in some way, shape or form, people have felt

18:16.000 --> 18:20.000
and experienced that deficit way of thinking towards them.

18:20.000 --> 18:23.000
But the thing that I would ask you to step into

18:23.000 --> 18:28.000
and to embrace myself included is to try and hit free,

18:28.000 --> 18:31.000
to try and hit free and to think about how we can work

18:31.000 --> 18:33.000
in kind of collaborative circumstances,

18:33.000 --> 18:37.000
how we can work in terms of these kind of collaborative pedagogies.

18:37.000 --> 18:40.000
You know, team teaching is not novel,

18:40.000 --> 18:42.000
but I think the ways we do it,

18:42.000 --> 18:46.000
that peer engagement, that collegiality, in these spaces,

18:46.000 --> 18:48.000
it has become lost.

18:48.000 --> 18:50.000
And one of the reasons it's become lost actually

18:50.000 --> 18:53.000
is not necessary to do with pedagogy anymore.

18:53.000 --> 18:57.000
It's to do with something that is rewarded really handsomely

18:57.000 --> 18:59.000
in academia and that's kind of individualism.

18:59.000 --> 19:02.000
There's a vanity and an individualism that is rewarding

19:02.000 --> 19:05.000
in academia and it actually isolates us.

19:05.000 --> 19:09.000
It completely disrupts our charge and it does the absolute opposite

19:09.000 --> 19:13.000
of engaging as a collective.

19:13.000 --> 19:17.000
And in such divisive times within a societal

19:17.000 --> 19:19.000
political and economical context,

19:19.000 --> 19:22.000
the need for us to be collective

19:22.000 --> 19:23.000
has never been more important,

19:23.000 --> 19:27.000
particularly in these collegiate pedagogical spaces.

19:27.000 --> 19:30.000
And as my mom always says,

19:30.000 --> 19:31.000
the word to wise is enough,

19:31.000 --> 19:33.000
so I want to bore you to tears after lunch,

19:33.000 --> 19:37.000
but just I would urge you to kind of think about

19:37.000 --> 19:40.000
that if you think of what is the greatest vision or image

19:40.000 --> 19:45.000
you can think of in terms of when you see someone completely happy.

19:45.000 --> 19:48.000
For me, like I said, it's a kid in the park.

19:48.000 --> 19:50.000
And if I think back to being a kid myself,

19:50.000 --> 19:52.000
I think I was at my happiest when I was in the park

19:52.000 --> 19:54.000
staring into the sun and when you stare into the sun,

19:54.000 --> 19:56.000
it's like coderizing, it's sort of red.

19:56.000 --> 19:58.000
You're like, this is bliss.

19:58.000 --> 20:01.000
And you think to yourself, how do I get that feeling back?

20:01.000 --> 20:06.000
And it's about really how we are in these spaces.

20:06.000 --> 20:08.000
And embracing it.

20:08.000 --> 20:10.000
And I guess that idea of failure.

20:10.000 --> 20:13.000
You know, I had to chew at college.

20:13.000 --> 20:16.000
You always used to say, like, you should respect failure,

20:16.000 --> 20:19.000
but you should never be paralyzed by it.

20:19.000 --> 20:23.000
So don't be afraid to fail in these pedagogical spaces.

20:23.000 --> 20:26.000
Don't be afraid to try new things.

20:26.000 --> 20:29.000
Don't be afraid to work collaboratively

20:29.000 --> 20:31.000
and work towards

20:31.000 --> 20:33.000
this side of collective empowerment.

20:33.000 --> 20:37.000
Empowering people in these educational spaces right now.

20:37.000 --> 20:39.000
Well, it's always been since time and more in,

20:39.000 --> 20:42.000
but even more importantly now, I think that is the greatest gift

20:42.000 --> 20:44.000
that one can give a person.

20:44.000 --> 20:46.000
I was going to make a reference to building the car,

20:46.000 --> 20:48.000
but I don't want to speak too much.

20:48.000 --> 20:50.000
So thank you.

20:50.000 --> 20:52.000
I really, really appreciate your time.

20:52.000 --> 20:54.000
I'm always mindful of talking at people.

20:54.000 --> 20:56.000
I'd rather talk with people.

20:56.000 --> 20:58.000
I appreciate all of you.

20:58.000 --> 21:01.000
And if nothing else, you've heard the rumblings of a madman

21:01.000 --> 21:02.000
from South London.

21:02.000 --> 21:03.000
So hello, goodbye.

21:03.000 --> 21:04.000
And I appreciate it.

21:04.000 --> 21:05.000
Thank you.

21:05.000 --> 21:06.000
Cheers.

21:06.000 --> 21:15.000
Thank you so much.

21:15.000 --> 21:18.000
And that does leave us with plenty of time for that space

21:18.000 --> 21:22.000
for, again, connection, engagement, discussion together.

21:22.000 --> 21:25.000
So I have the mic, and I'll bring it around.

21:25.000 --> 21:27.000
Would anybody like to?

21:27.000 --> 21:28.000
Yes, I can see.

21:28.000 --> 21:29.000
Hands up there.

21:42.000 --> 21:43.000
Thank you, Kerry.

21:43.000 --> 21:44.000
I didn't expect to be first.

21:44.000 --> 21:45.000
Thank you, Jason.

21:45.000 --> 21:49.000
That was really, very empowering.

21:49.000 --> 21:51.000
I came here today to be a bit well-nourished,

21:51.000 --> 21:53.000
and I feel a bit more nourished.

21:53.000 --> 21:55.000
So thank you very much for that.

21:55.000 --> 21:56.000
A big question to you.

21:56.000 --> 22:00.000
You're speaking very broadly and bravely

22:00.000 --> 22:04.000
about our needs to really think about the profession

22:04.000 --> 22:06.000
and what we've come here to do.

22:06.000 --> 22:11.000
How do leaders manage that against the complexities

22:11.000 --> 22:12.000
that you've spoken about?

22:12.000 --> 22:15.000
What do we now need to do in a practical way

22:15.000 --> 22:20.000
to help our colleagues to hit free?

22:20.000 --> 22:21.000
That's great.

22:21.000 --> 22:22.000
Thank you so much.

22:22.000 --> 22:25.000
I appreciate it.

22:25.000 --> 22:26.000
It's difficult.

22:26.000 --> 22:29.000
I think, from afar, I'll speak for myself.

22:29.000 --> 22:32.000
Sometimes I've been privately very critical of leaders.

22:32.000 --> 22:36.000
I feel like they have a huge amount of agency and power,

22:36.000 --> 22:40.000
and they don't always utilize that in the best ways.

22:40.000 --> 22:45.000
I think it was until I became a senior leader in higher education

22:45.000 --> 22:49.000
in a previous role, but I realised it's kind of easy to take

22:49.000 --> 22:54.000
pop shots when you don't have to do it.

22:54.000 --> 23:00.000
I think for leaders, one thing I do think is universal

23:00.000 --> 23:04.000
or should be more universal is leading bravely.

23:04.000 --> 23:09.000
I think leaders struggle to make the brave choices

23:09.000 --> 23:12.000
and part of the brave choice is around hitting free.

23:12.000 --> 23:17.000
It is around giving people as much freedom as possible

23:17.000 --> 23:20.000
to be able to exercise their art, because pedagogy

23:20.000 --> 23:23.000
in its raw recents is the art of teaching.

23:23.000 --> 23:26.000
That art has been sanitized a lot,

23:26.000 --> 23:30.000
and not always, but a significant amount of time

23:30.000 --> 23:38.000
that is because of the actions that senior leaders impose

23:38.000 --> 23:40.000
on their staff.

23:40.000 --> 23:43.000
The one thing I would always probably encourage senior leaders

23:43.000 --> 23:48.000
is to try and remember is that you're working with these individuals.

23:48.000 --> 23:50.000
They're not beholden to anyone.

23:50.000 --> 23:53.000
You're working with these individuals, and it's really important

23:53.000 --> 23:56.000
that you see them, and you recognise their capabilities

23:56.000 --> 23:59.000
and provide them with all the apparatus available

23:59.000 --> 24:02.000
to execute their charge to the best of their abilities.

24:02.000 --> 24:07.000
I think senior leaders are taking that away a bit more and more

24:07.000 --> 24:11.000
and more by distributing power ironically in the wrong places.

24:11.000 --> 24:13.000
And I think that's important.

24:13.000 --> 24:16.000
I think it's important for senior leaders to obviously hold power

24:16.000 --> 24:18.000
and they do effectively distribute it,

24:18.000 --> 24:21.000
but it's about who has access to that power,

24:21.000 --> 24:23.000
and it's about really where possible,

24:23.000 --> 24:26.000
eliminating hierarchy as much as possible.

24:26.000 --> 24:30.000
I don't believe in hierarchy, and I never have,

24:30.000 --> 24:33.000
so I've never been deferential to that.

24:33.000 --> 24:35.000
There's a need for hierarchy.

24:35.000 --> 24:37.000
I understand why it has to be there,

24:37.000 --> 24:41.000
but I also think leaders, senior leaders,

24:41.000 --> 24:45.000
recognise in their significant pressures that they're under

24:45.000 --> 24:48.000
do also need to tend to the people that they are working with

24:48.000 --> 24:49.000
because that's hugely important.

24:49.000 --> 24:51.000
And while we're on that subject actually,

24:51.000 --> 24:54.000
I think the mental health of staff is a really, really significant thing.

24:54.000 --> 24:57.000
I don't think we've ever been in a time where

24:57.000 --> 25:00.000
I'm in the process of collecting the data for this,

25:00.000 --> 25:03.000
and I don't ever make references to my own research,

25:03.000 --> 25:07.000
but the data that I'm collecting at the moment is to find out

25:07.000 --> 25:10.000
how many staff across the sector over the last three years

25:10.000 --> 25:14.000
have been on sick leave, and I haven't got the data in front of me

25:14.000 --> 25:17.000
but I'm in the process of getting that data from HESSA

25:17.000 --> 25:20.000
and other agencies, but it's significant.

25:20.000 --> 25:23.000
And I think that the mechanisms in place support staff

25:23.000 --> 25:28.000
while being a mental health have completely forgotten

25:28.000 --> 25:31.000
these kind of factories now.

25:31.000 --> 25:35.000
And I think senior leaders have a duty of care to their staff

25:35.000 --> 25:38.000
and I think by recognising that duty of care,

25:38.000 --> 25:41.000
they will actually have better outcomes, better pedagogical outcomes

25:41.000 --> 25:45.000
for those staff, and most importantly, the students.

25:45.000 --> 25:47.000
You're welcome. Thank you.

25:47.000 --> 25:49.000
Thank you, Jason.

25:49.000 --> 25:54.000
Like my colleague, I also found your talk very nurturing and comforting.

25:54.000 --> 25:57.000
And I really appreciated how you, I feel like you've modeled

25:57.000 --> 25:59.000
on the ability you've talked about making mistakes

25:59.000 --> 26:02.000
you've spoken with outside. I really appreciate seeing that model.

26:02.000 --> 26:04.000
So thank you. And I just wanted to ask you,

26:04.000 --> 26:06.000
how do you manage it on a personal level

26:06.000 --> 26:09.000
because you feel like you're bringing

26:09.000 --> 26:11.000
and you're encouraging us to bring our whole cells

26:11.000 --> 26:13.000
and there is a cost involved in that?

26:13.000 --> 26:15.000
Well, that's a great question.

26:19.000 --> 26:21.000
Shall I answer honestly?

26:21.000 --> 26:26.000
I feel I'm scared.

26:26.000 --> 26:31.000
And the reason I'm scared is not because I think students are dangerous.

26:31.000 --> 26:34.000
I just think we're living in dangerous times.

26:34.000 --> 26:40.000
And I think there are so many things that arrest

26:40.000 --> 26:44.000
educators to be able to do what they really want to do in spaces.

26:44.000 --> 26:48.000
Regardless of where I sit on the political spectrum,

26:48.000 --> 26:53.000
I just think that everybody is finding it hard for lots of different reasons.

26:53.000 --> 26:58.000
I think the reasons I find it hard

26:58.000 --> 27:05.000
and I think many people find it hard is because I genuinely think

27:05.000 --> 27:10.000
that you are one error away from losing your job.

27:10.000 --> 27:14.000
And I don't think that's a particularly nice space to be in.

27:14.000 --> 27:19.000
And I don't think it encourages people to be free in that space.

27:19.000 --> 27:22.000
In a way that is not harmful to other people.

27:22.000 --> 27:25.000
And if you spend too much time kind of thinking about something like that,

27:25.000 --> 27:30.000
you just don't flow. You don't think everything becomes very structured,

27:30.000 --> 27:35.000
very regimented, and very prescriptive by its very nature.

27:35.000 --> 27:38.000
And I guess I just worry about that.

27:38.000 --> 27:43.000
I worry about that a lot because you're worried about making mistakes.

27:43.000 --> 27:47.000
And when I first started teaching, when I started as a peer teacher,

27:47.000 --> 27:50.000
I never had that. Like, I never had that.

27:50.000 --> 27:53.000
I was just, I was free in my head.

27:53.000 --> 27:55.000
I knew exactly what I needed to do.

27:55.000 --> 27:57.000
I knew exactly how I wanted to do it.

27:57.000 --> 28:01.000
And then you kind of, and even the beginning of my career in higher education.

28:01.000 --> 28:08.000
But then weirdly, the more agency you got as you kind of were doing,

28:08.000 --> 28:10.000
you were going up the ladder.

28:10.000 --> 28:14.000
You feel that that's the more kind of jeopardy you feel.

28:14.000 --> 28:18.000
You feel like if I say something wrong here, it's hugely consequential.

28:18.000 --> 28:24.000
And given the nature of the work that many others do across the intersection,

28:24.000 --> 28:28.000
including myself, it's a really precarious time to be in.

28:28.000 --> 28:32.000
And I've known many people who have said something in class,

28:32.000 --> 28:37.000
and have been given a verbal warning,

28:37.000 --> 28:42.000
or a secondary warning for a gross misconduct, and have found themselves out of work.

28:42.000 --> 28:45.000
And I'm not exaggerating when I say that.

28:45.000 --> 28:48.000
And by all accounts, what they're saying is, you know,

28:48.000 --> 28:51.000
they're relating to either aspect of their research,

28:51.000 --> 28:54.000
or they're talking about a phenomenon in terms of, you know,

28:54.000 --> 28:58.000
intersectional inequality, whether it be racism, sexism, homophobia,

28:58.000 --> 29:03.000
ableism, you know, religious inequality, all those types of things.

29:04.000 --> 29:07.000
Yeah, I try to rest that.

29:07.000 --> 29:09.000
And I try and go for it.

29:09.000 --> 29:11.000
I try and go for it as much as possible.

29:11.000 --> 29:18.000
I think the thing that's different now is that everything...

29:18.000 --> 29:21.000
I never really said too many wrong things before,

29:21.000 --> 29:25.000
but I think the difference is over the last three years,

29:25.000 --> 29:27.000
everything you say is of consequence.

29:27.000 --> 29:32.000
I always knew that, but I never really felt it

29:32.000 --> 29:37.000
where as now I'm like, you say one thing you're going to nup in the telegraph from page.

29:37.000 --> 29:39.000
And that's my fun.

29:39.000 --> 29:41.000
I think having to think in that way,

29:41.000 --> 29:44.000
but I don't think that's exclusive to me.

29:44.000 --> 29:47.000
I think that's everybody in this room in some way, shape or form.

29:47.000 --> 29:49.000
And it's difficult.

29:49.000 --> 29:50.000
It's difficult.

29:50.000 --> 29:55.000
But, you know, when a kid goes into a park and they fall off like

29:55.000 --> 29:58.000
the kind of climbing bars or a climbing frame,

29:58.000 --> 30:01.000
you know, it doesn't perturb them from going back on it.

30:01.000 --> 30:02.000
And I love that.

30:02.000 --> 30:03.000
I love that.

30:03.000 --> 30:05.000
You know, as an adult, if we fell off something now,

30:05.000 --> 30:07.000
the likelihood is like, I'm not doing that again,

30:07.000 --> 30:09.000
because I don't want to get hurt.

30:09.000 --> 30:10.000
But with the kid, they just...

30:10.000 --> 30:13.000
They're just like, okay, this hurt, but I'm going to do it again.

30:13.000 --> 30:15.000
And I guess what I'm saying is I don't want us to...

30:15.000 --> 30:19.000
I want us to try and find that in pedagogical spaces.

30:19.000 --> 30:21.000
They still didn't go so well,

30:21.000 --> 30:23.000
but what are the learnings I can take from it

30:23.000 --> 30:25.000
and how can I do that differently?

30:25.000 --> 30:27.000
And I think that difference can happen

30:27.000 --> 30:28.000
when you're working with people.

30:28.000 --> 30:30.000
Is that how I have safety in numbers?

30:30.000 --> 30:32.000
The collective empowerment and empowering

30:32.000 --> 30:35.000
and enriching each other, I think is so, so important

30:35.000 --> 30:38.000
because in this sector, you know,

30:38.000 --> 30:41.000
there are people who behave in ways that I think are really

30:41.000 --> 30:43.000
abhorrent and unacceptable,

30:43.000 --> 30:46.000
but ultimately we're all we have in this sector.

30:46.000 --> 30:49.000
And I think it's really incumbent on us to look after one another

30:49.000 --> 30:52.000
and to enrich one another and to really pour into each other.

30:52.000 --> 30:55.000
And I think that's how you do it.

30:55.000 --> 30:57.000
You're welcome.

30:57.000 --> 30:58.000
I'm sorry.

30:58.000 --> 30:59.000
I'm sorry.

30:59.000 --> 31:00.000
I'm sorry.

31:00.000 --> 31:01.000
I'm sorry.

31:01.000 --> 31:02.000
I'm sorry.

31:02.000 --> 31:03.000
I'm sorry.

31:03.000 --> 31:04.000
I'm sorry.

31:04.000 --> 31:05.000
I'm sorry.

31:05.000 --> 31:06.000
I'm sorry.

31:06.000 --> 31:07.000
I'm sorry.

31:07.000 --> 31:08.000
I'll pass it over.

31:08.000 --> 31:09.000
Here.

31:09.000 --> 31:10.000
Here.

31:10.000 --> 31:11.000
Here.

31:11.000 --> 31:12.000
Here.

31:12.000 --> 31:13.000
Here.

31:13.000 --> 31:14.000
Here.

31:14.000 --> 31:15.000
Here.

31:15.000 --> 31:16.000
Here.

31:16.000 --> 31:17.000
Thank you.

31:17.000 --> 31:18.000
Hi, Jason.

31:18.000 --> 31:19.000
My name's Amanda.

31:19.000 --> 31:23.000
I was really struck by your comment about failing and failing

31:23.000 --> 31:24.000
safely.

31:24.000 --> 31:27.000
And I think one of the things that I would be keen to hear from you is

31:27.000 --> 31:31.000
how do we create spaces where students can fail safely.

31:31.000 --> 31:33.000
I think given the pressures, particularly in Russell Group

31:33.000 --> 31:36.000
institutions, in post-as-indrome, value for money with

31:36.000 --> 31:39.000
degrees, there's a lot of pressure on us and our students to

31:39.000 --> 31:41.000
just be the best all the time.

31:41.000 --> 31:44.000
And actually sometimes failing in a safe environment is just as

31:44.000 --> 31:45.000
valuable.

31:45.000 --> 31:48.000
So I guess your thoughts on how do we encourage that and how do

31:48.000 --> 31:49.000
we bring that out of our students?

31:49.000 --> 31:51.000
I think that's a great question.

31:51.000 --> 31:58.000
And in a word, I think probably humility and vulnerability.

31:58.000 --> 32:04.000
I know there's a foreign concept in academia, but I think

32:04.000 --> 32:10.000
humility is hugely important in those spaces and vulnerability.

32:10.000 --> 32:17.000
I often tell students not to placate them, but to tell them as

32:17.000 --> 32:22.000
the truth that you guys are much better than I'll ever be,

32:22.000 --> 32:23.000
which is the truth.

32:23.000 --> 32:26.000
There's no shame in conceding that.

32:26.000 --> 32:31.000
I've obviously done okay in the area that I specialised in,

32:31.000 --> 32:33.000
but there's no shame for me.

32:33.000 --> 32:35.000
I'm speaking for myself in terms of conceitations.

32:35.000 --> 32:36.000
You know, more than me.

32:36.000 --> 32:38.000
So part of this is that we learn from one another.

32:38.000 --> 32:39.000
We learn from one another.

32:39.000 --> 32:42.000
It's not about one upmanship or trying to show off or

32:42.000 --> 32:45.000
you know, certain swinging exercises.

32:45.000 --> 32:52.000
It's about learning and that can happen between all of us.

32:52.000 --> 32:56.000
And I think having the humility to exercise that humility

32:56.000 --> 33:00.000
in the first place, it gives the students, in many respects,

33:00.000 --> 33:03.000
a licence to feel vulnerable to be able to fail.

33:03.000 --> 33:06.000
Also given them kind of context, which is that,

33:06.000 --> 33:09.000
particularly I guess, maybe in rust group context, this isn't

33:09.000 --> 33:11.000
everything.

33:11.000 --> 33:12.000
This isn't everything.

33:12.000 --> 33:15.000
And that is something that I tell students very often.

33:15.000 --> 33:17.000
This isn't everything.

33:17.000 --> 33:21.000
Like, the things that I think students should focus on,

33:21.000 --> 33:24.000
the first and most important thing is being a good human being.

33:24.000 --> 33:29.000
And unfortunately, I don't always think that that lesson

33:29.000 --> 33:33.000
is modelled very well in academia because it's very nature

33:33.000 --> 33:36.000
around being individualistic, the vanity aspect,

33:36.000 --> 33:41.000
people treat people by and large pretty badly.

33:41.000 --> 33:46.000
For not really any good reason that I could fathom

33:46.000 --> 33:47.000
or construct.

33:47.000 --> 33:51.000
And I think it's important to model to students how important

33:51.000 --> 33:54.000
it is to treat one another with respect, with dignity,

33:54.000 --> 33:55.000
create spaces belonging.

33:55.000 --> 33:57.000
And part of that is modelling that.

33:57.000 --> 34:00.000
And we can do that by one, being humble in these spaces

34:00.000 --> 34:03.000
and recognising that we don't know everything.

34:03.000 --> 34:07.000
Two, eliminating hierarchy as much as it's possible to do so.

34:07.000 --> 34:09.000
And look, I totally understand that there's privilege

34:09.000 --> 34:12.000
to attach that because there are certain people who can't actually

34:12.000 --> 34:17.000
they have to exert a form of not hierarchy authority

34:17.000 --> 34:21.000
because they are positioned as deficit in the first place.

34:21.000 --> 34:24.000
But I think where possible, I think one of the things

34:24.000 --> 34:27.000
that can disarm people, I think most people is kindness.

34:27.000 --> 34:30.000
And I do think the thing that disarms just about everybody

34:30.000 --> 34:33.000
in my opinion, which is more to do my upbringing,

34:33.000 --> 34:34.000
is humility.

34:34.000 --> 34:37.000
And if you bring kind of vulnerability into that space,

34:37.000 --> 34:39.000
I think it's a really powerful elixir.

34:39.000 --> 34:42.000
And I think if students see that and it's like,

34:42.000 --> 34:43.000
look, it's good to fail.

34:43.000 --> 34:44.000
It's good to fail.

34:44.000 --> 34:46.000
The reason why it's good to fail is because you will learn from it.

34:46.000 --> 34:49.000
You will learn nothing from being successful all the time.

34:49.000 --> 34:50.000
It's good to learn that.

34:50.000 --> 34:54.000
And if we can model that as pedagogues in these spaces,

34:54.000 --> 34:58.000
I think it's a nonbelievable job in very, very trying times

34:58.000 --> 35:02.000
because it's about creating obviously really good students

35:02.000 --> 35:05.000
but the most important thing, the central, most important thing

35:05.000 --> 35:09.000
is about creating or contributing towards creating good human beings.

35:09.000 --> 35:15.000
And I think my whole thing was I'll never be the best academic in the world

35:15.000 --> 35:17.000
when I'm close to it.

35:17.000 --> 35:21.000
But what I can aspire to be is the nicest academic in the world

35:21.000 --> 35:25.000
or the kindness that that's a great aspiration to have, not that I am.

35:25.000 --> 35:27.000
But it's a good aspiration to have.

35:27.000 --> 35:30.000
And I tell students that because very often they'll ask me,

35:30.000 --> 35:32.000
what did you do to get to where you got to?

35:32.000 --> 35:35.000
And I always explained to them, it's not about getting there.

35:35.000 --> 35:38.000
It's the way you did it. Did you bring people with you?

35:38.000 --> 35:44.000
Did you treat people with respect where you recognized the people that came before you?

35:44.000 --> 35:48.000
That created the pathway for you to be able to traverse the path around.

35:48.000 --> 35:50.000
It's these types of things that matter.

35:50.000 --> 35:53.000
And the rest of it kind of follows in suit.

35:53.000 --> 35:57.000
It's what I do, and it's sort of touched with it's sort of work for me.

35:57.000 --> 36:01.000
So you seem like you've got an amazing handle on it, but hopefully that might help.

36:01.000 --> 36:03.000
You're welcome.

36:03.000 --> 36:14.000
Hi, really great touch speech. I absolutely loved it.

36:14.000 --> 36:19.000
One question I did have was you talked around kind of enabling,

36:19.000 --> 36:22.000
not assuming people have the knowledge that they would have the knowledge.

36:22.000 --> 36:27.000
One question I had was, what if by doing that you're ostracizing students?

36:27.000 --> 36:29.000
So you've pushed, you've assumed.

36:29.000 --> 36:31.000
Can I just, can you hear me?

36:31.000 --> 36:32.000
Yeah, I can, sorry.

36:32.000 --> 36:34.000
What do you do?

36:34.000 --> 36:36.000
Oh, is it for the, okay.

36:36.000 --> 36:39.000
Well, I'll yell and try and not to break the mic at the same time.

36:39.000 --> 36:43.000
So what do you do when you're by assuming people know what they're doing,

36:43.000 --> 36:46.000
or that they're the smartest people in the room, that you inadvertently ostracize them,

36:46.000 --> 36:49.000
because they don't know, and then you've gone, but you do know,

36:49.000 --> 36:52.000
and then they feel like they can't show that vulnerability,

36:52.000 --> 36:53.000
because you've made that assumption.

36:53.000 --> 36:55.000
That's a great question. I really appreciate that.

36:55.000 --> 36:58.000
So this gives me a chance to explain that actually.

36:58.000 --> 37:04.000
So, I think the thing is you create a space for learning.

37:04.000 --> 37:08.000
So part of me making the assumption, not that I necessarily say it,

37:08.000 --> 37:11.000
I make the assumption, which is a positive assumption,

37:11.000 --> 37:16.000
for people to be able to feel those, though they can engage and ask questions in that space.

37:16.000 --> 37:20.000
If I give the impression that people don't know,

37:20.000 --> 37:23.000
my feeling has always been that you can suppress what people know,

37:23.000 --> 37:25.000
and actually position yourself as knowing more.

37:25.000 --> 37:30.000
On top of that, I guess the feeling, or the philosophy,

37:30.000 --> 37:33.000
comes from my own lived experience,

37:33.000 --> 37:36.000
and that of many people of color in particular,

37:36.000 --> 37:39.000
which is that when you walk into a space,

37:39.000 --> 37:42.000
people just never think you are who you are.

37:42.000 --> 37:44.000
And because I know what that feels like,

37:44.000 --> 37:48.000
I know what that feels like, and I know which I never ever be on.

37:48.000 --> 37:52.000
I always work with the most, you know, there's a generosity

37:52.000 --> 37:57.000
in working with the perception or the belief or the philosophy

37:57.000 --> 38:01.000
that somebody may know, in creating and facilitating a space,

38:01.000 --> 38:05.000
where they can exhibit that knowledge in a way that is

38:05.000 --> 38:10.000
collective, collegiate, and kind of embraces everyone.

38:10.000 --> 38:13.000
You know, as opposed to being the person in the room

38:13.000 --> 38:15.000
that you just make an assumption on.

38:15.000 --> 38:19.000
And so I've often tried to work with that premise in my mind,

38:19.000 --> 38:23.000
because, like I said, I've been the person that people do that to,

38:23.000 --> 38:25.000
and that's not unique to me.

38:25.000 --> 38:29.000
There are many, you know, up until maybe three years ago,

38:29.000 --> 38:33.000
you'd go to places and, you know, people would be like,

38:33.000 --> 38:35.000
I'm looking for Jason.

38:35.000 --> 38:36.000
I'm Jason.

38:36.000 --> 38:42.000
You know, I think it's an old, it's a tried and tested one,

38:42.000 --> 38:43.000
but it's true.

38:43.000 --> 38:45.000
I've lost count at a number.

38:45.000 --> 38:47.000
I'm using people of colour for this particular example,

38:47.000 --> 38:52.000
but I've lost count at a number of people who say to me,

38:52.000 --> 38:55.000
so someone comes in the room, and they say,

38:55.000 --> 38:57.000
sorry, you decline.

38:57.000 --> 39:00.000
I'm just about to, I'm just, I've got a lecture,

39:00.000 --> 39:03.000
and they'll say, no, no, I'm X, Y, and Z.

39:03.000 --> 39:07.000
And, by the way, there's absolutely, you know,

39:07.000 --> 39:11.000
the contribution that domestic staff make to these institutions

39:11.000 --> 39:14.000
to argue more important than anything we do.

39:14.000 --> 39:17.000
Much more important in my eyes, especially coming from a family

39:17.000 --> 39:20.000
of people who have cleaned myself and cleared it.

39:20.000 --> 39:23.000
But what is interesting is how people position these things

39:23.000 --> 39:26.000
as a hierarchy in their mind, and they see that as a deficit

39:26.000 --> 39:29.000
approach to work to, you know.

39:29.000 --> 39:35.000
So, if someone says, you know, oh, I thought you were a student,

39:35.000 --> 39:38.000
you know, some days you're like, I'll take that,

39:38.000 --> 39:42.000
and some days you're like, I'm looking about 50 today,

39:42.000 --> 39:44.000
so I don't accept that.

39:44.000 --> 39:47.000
And by the way, that positioning is always interesting

39:47.000 --> 39:51.000
because you kind of know when that's happening.

39:51.000 --> 39:55.000
So, I think that generosity is hugely important,

39:55.000 --> 39:57.000
and I think, like I said, for a lot of people who've been

39:57.000 --> 39:59.000
on the end across the intersection,

39:59.000 --> 40:02.000
might I add, of that kind of deficit way of thinking,

40:02.000 --> 40:05.000
that I feel like the greatest gift I could give to someone

40:05.000 --> 40:09.000
is to presume that you are whole and that you know.

40:09.000 --> 40:13.000
And then give you the opportunity to engage in that learning

40:13.000 --> 40:16.000
because in that sense, you're starting from here,

40:16.000 --> 40:18.000
and you can kind of, you can choose where you put yourself

40:18.000 --> 40:19.000
on that scale.

40:19.000 --> 40:22.000
But if I start you from here, you know, your opportunity

40:22.000 --> 40:25.000
to show me any different would be based purely

40:25.000 --> 40:29.000
on my conscious or unconscious biases.

40:29.000 --> 40:31.000
So, that's what I try and do.

40:31.000 --> 40:34.000
That's not to say it's a hard science, and it works all the time.

40:34.000 --> 40:36.000
It's kind of a work in progress.

40:36.000 --> 40:40.000
It has been since I was 25, some don't have 16 years.

40:40.000 --> 40:43.000
So, yeah, you're always trying new and different things,

40:43.000 --> 40:48.000
but I think it's a huge kind of respect that I'm showing

40:48.000 --> 40:51.000
to people when I presume that they know,

40:51.000 --> 40:53.000
because I mean it from the bottom of my heart,

40:53.000 --> 40:55.000
and I mean it with the best of intent,

40:55.000 --> 40:58.000
because I think it's unkind when you look at someone

40:58.000 --> 41:01.000
and make a judgment and you're like, you just don't know.

41:01.000 --> 41:03.000
You don't know to look at somebody, you know,

41:03.000 --> 41:05.000
and I know which judgment I would prefer

41:05.000 --> 41:07.000
if I had to make one.

41:07.000 --> 41:09.000
Jason.

41:09.000 --> 41:11.000
Yeah, Jason.

41:11.000 --> 41:13.000
Thank you.

41:13.000 --> 41:14.000
Thank you.

41:14.000 --> 41:15.000
Thank you.

41:15.000 --> 41:17.000
I love your radical humility and pedagogy of love

41:17.000 --> 41:19.000
and all those kind of things.

41:19.000 --> 41:21.000
I mean, you mentioned intelligence,

41:21.000 --> 41:23.000
and you said there's lots of people

41:23.000 --> 41:25.000
who are intelligent than you.

41:25.000 --> 41:27.000
I kind of feel like that as well,

41:27.000 --> 41:29.000
and as far as the question for me is this issue

41:29.000 --> 41:31.000
of imposter syndrome.

41:31.000 --> 41:35.000
And I know that it can affect anybody and everybody,

41:35.000 --> 41:37.000
but in a place like what if I think it probably,

41:37.000 --> 41:40.000
you know, ironically I think it affects a lot of students

41:40.000 --> 41:43.000
because I think that in a place that's possibly the best

41:43.000 --> 41:45.000
and the cleverest,

41:45.000 --> 41:47.000
do we need to abolish the concept of intelligence?

41:47.000 --> 41:50.000
Is there a violent concept that's implicated in

41:50.000 --> 41:52.000
the reality,

41:52.000 --> 41:54.000
especially disabled people,

41:54.000 --> 41:57.000
or Black people of women?

41:57.000 --> 41:58.000
A few years ago,

41:58.000 --> 41:59.000
we had a component,

41:59.000 --> 42:02.000
what if it was called intelligence squared after you remember that?

42:02.000 --> 42:04.000
What if you can double your intelligence or something?

42:04.000 --> 42:06.000
Right.

42:06.000 --> 42:09.000
So it's just a kind of thought about imposter syndrome

42:09.000 --> 42:13.000
and what reproduces that in these kind of institutions.

42:13.000 --> 42:16.000
One is so great.

42:16.000 --> 42:19.000
LAUGHTER

42:19.000 --> 42:21.000
So...

42:21.000 --> 42:24.000
LAUGHTER

42:24.000 --> 42:26.000
One,

42:26.000 --> 42:27.000
Gernam, thank you so much.

42:27.000 --> 42:28.000
I can't,

42:28.000 --> 42:33.000
without embarrassing professor Gernam seeing a current

42:33.000 --> 42:36.000
emphasize how influential his work has been

42:36.000 --> 42:40.000
for a lot of people from Black and Brown.

42:40.000 --> 42:42.000
I think minority backgrounds who have tried to do work

42:42.000 --> 42:44.000
in this space,

42:44.000 --> 42:46.000
and it's crazy because they kind of say

42:46.000 --> 42:48.000
you should never meet your heroes and people you look up to.

42:48.000 --> 42:52.000
But, you know, when I first met Gernam

42:52.000 --> 42:55.000
pretty 12, 10 years ago,

42:55.000 --> 42:57.000
the same person I met is,

42:57.000 --> 43:00.000
and I hoped he would be the same person he is now.

43:00.000 --> 43:03.000
And when you see more examples where that doesn't happen,

43:03.000 --> 43:05.000
you truly realize how special that is.

43:05.000 --> 43:06.000
So firstly, thank you for that.

43:06.000 --> 43:08.000
I really appreciate you.

43:08.000 --> 43:11.000
And just about everyone who's come up with me

43:11.000 --> 43:13.000
does as well.

43:13.000 --> 43:15.000
In terms of the imposter syndrome,

43:15.000 --> 43:17.000
I think it's...

43:17.000 --> 43:19.000
It's always a really interesting thing.

43:19.000 --> 43:21.000
My whole thing about the intelligence thing

43:21.000 --> 43:23.000
is that I do think it's a colonial hangover.

43:23.000 --> 43:26.000
I do think it's a colonial concept I agree with you.

43:26.000 --> 43:30.000
I guess it depends what people add currency to.

43:30.000 --> 43:32.000
So...

43:32.000 --> 43:34.000
I don't, I'm not intelligent,

43:34.000 --> 43:37.000
but what I do have is wisdom.

43:37.000 --> 43:40.000
And I also have the wisdom to know

43:40.000 --> 43:43.000
that, and this is just my personal belief,

43:43.000 --> 43:45.000
I don't think,

43:45.000 --> 43:48.000
and I hope I don't sound like a narcissist.

43:48.000 --> 43:51.000
I don't suffer from imposter syndrome.

43:51.000 --> 43:53.000
And the reason why I don't is because I believe

43:53.000 --> 43:55.000
where I ended up is where I'm supposed to be.

43:55.000 --> 43:58.000
And I also was taught from a very young age,

43:58.000 --> 44:01.000
knowing that I didn't have any academic acumen whatsoever,

44:01.000 --> 44:04.000
that skill can be measured,

44:04.000 --> 44:06.000
but will is indomitable.

44:06.000 --> 44:08.000
So I knew that will couldn't...

44:08.000 --> 44:09.000
It's not something you can measure.

44:09.000 --> 44:12.000
It's just about giving it everything you've got

44:12.000 --> 44:17.000
and admittedly along the way I got very, very lucky.

44:17.000 --> 44:20.000
And I think the thing that I've always worked towards

44:20.000 --> 44:24.000
is to ensure that luck doesn't come into it.

44:24.000 --> 44:26.000
In other words, you know,

44:26.000 --> 44:30.000
I want more people to be where they're supposed to be.

44:30.000 --> 44:33.000
It's not about kind of these sliding doors moments

44:33.000 --> 44:34.000
and stuff like that.

44:34.000 --> 44:36.000
And yes, I worked as hard as I could,

44:36.000 --> 44:38.000
but there's people who've worked just as hard,

44:38.000 --> 44:40.000
if not harder, who have not had yield,

44:40.000 --> 44:43.000
who have not been yielded or gifted the same rewards.

44:43.000 --> 44:47.000
So you recognize very quickly that, yes, in some ways,

44:47.000 --> 44:51.000
I have closed the gap by working as hard as I can.

44:51.000 --> 44:53.000
So I don't feel imposter syndrome in the same way,

44:53.000 --> 44:57.000
but I also recognize that the work to be done

44:57.000 --> 45:00.000
is to ensure that actually people feel this sense

45:00.000 --> 45:02.000
of not entitlement, but this sense of,

45:02.000 --> 45:04.000
I do deserve to be here.

45:04.000 --> 45:09.000
And a huge part of that comes from diversifying the space

45:09.000 --> 45:12.000
and trying even harder to ensure that there are more people

45:12.000 --> 45:14.000
in space as well as one or two,

45:14.000 --> 45:16.000
because that is not collective empowerment.

45:16.000 --> 45:18.000
That is individualistic.

45:20.000 --> 45:21.000
Thank you.

45:21.000 --> 45:23.000
Thank you.

45:23.000 --> 45:25.000
Thank you.
